Spec: Project/Model

Purpose:
  Define how the real software project is represented inside the SDD
  project model — the Project > Domain > Module > Feature > Component
  hierarchy AI resolves before touching source code. This file owns the
  SHAPE of that hierarchy only; the concrete instances (which domains,
  which modules) live in their own sibling files, not here (see
  DoesNotOwn, NavigationFiles).

  This SHAPE is fixed and reusable (bax Portability), but populating it
  is not mandatory in full for every project — HOW MUCH of it gets
  populated, with what architecture, is decided by the instantiation
  pipeline, not assumed here (bax ./instantiation.sdd -> CorePrinciple,
  "PROJECT IS GENERATED, NOT PREDEFINED").


Owns:
  Project structure (Project > Domain > Module > Feature > Component)
  Feature model (schema)
  Component model (schema)
  Ownership rule
  Project-model navigation order
  Project profile schema (Stack/Scale/Availability/Regions/Compliance/
    Constraints/Goals)
  Non-goals schema (explicitly excluded scope)


DoesNotOwn:
  Domain list and domain-level rules        (see ./domains.sdd)
  Module list and module-level contracts    (see ./modules.sdd)
  Routing/index map                         (see ./map.sdd)
  Concrete dependency graph                 (see ./dependencies.sdd)
  Category-level index declarations         (see ./indexes.sdd)
  This project's concrete architecture      (see ./architecture.sdd)
  This project's execution flow             (see ./flows.sdd)
  How a project instance gets decided       (see ./instantiation.sdd)
  Global SDD engine rules (chains/tasks/decisions/state/skills/prompts)

  (This file sits one level above domains.sdd/modules.sdd: those two
  already model the Domain and Module layers concretely — bax their own
  Spec headers. This file adds the two layers that were still
  unmodeled, Feature and Component, and states how all five layers
  relate, without re-declaring what domains.sdd/modules.sdd already own.)


Principle:

  The project model MUST represent the software system without
  requiring AI to inspect the entire source tree before understanding
  the system.

  AI MUST select best practices based on project context (stack, scale,
  constraints, non-goals), not apply generic best practices universally
  (bax ../architecture/principles.sdd -> ArchitectureSelection,
  ../project/instantiation.sdd -> SkillFiltering).


Profile:

  A project instance MAY declare its operational context so AI can
  choose architecture, skills, and implementation strategies that fit
  the actual project rather than a generic default. This file defines
  the schema only — concrete values belong to the instantiated project
  (bax ../instantiation.sdd -> Pipeline -> Project Instance).

  Schema:

    Stack:
      BE:    <backend framework / language>
      DB:    <database>
      FE:    <frontend framework>
      MD:    <mobile framework>
      DO:    <deployment / orchestration>

    Scale:
      DAU:   <number>
      MAU:   <number>
      QPS:   <number>

    Availability:
      SLA:   <percentage>

    Architecture:
      <style: modular_monolith / microservices / serverless / ...>

    Regions:
      <count>

    Compliance:
      <standards: GDPR / SOC2 / ...>

    Constraints:
      <list>

    Goals:
      <list>

    ComplexityBudget:
      <L0-L5 scale constraint that gates which patterns are eligible>

    NFR:
      Latency:     <target>
      Availability: <target>
      Security:     <baseline>
      Recovery:     <RPO/RTO>

    SecurityRiskBudget:
      SecurityLevel:     <L0-L5>
      RiskTolerance:     <low/medium/high>
      CriticalAllowed:   <true/false>
      HighAllowed:       <true/false>
      PentestRequired:   <true/false>
      ExternalExposure:  <public/internal/private>


NonGoals:

  A project instance MAY declare explicit non-goals to prevent AI from
  introducing scope creep via "best practice" additions the project has
  consciously excluded. This file defines the schema only — concrete
  values belong to the instantiated project.

  Schema:

    NonGoals:
      <explicitly excluded concept>

  Examples (illustrative only):
    microservices, multi-region, blockchain, event-sourcing


ProjectStructure:

  Project > Domain > Module > Feature > Component


Domain:

  A domain represents a coherent business capability.
  Schema and concrete domains: see ./domains.sdd.


Module:

  A module represents a technical or functional boundary inside a
  domain — what it owns, its public surface, its declared dependencies.
  Schema and concrete modules: see ./modules.sdd.


Feature:

  A feature represents executable product behavior inside a module —
  the smallest unit a prompt or task typically targets.

  Example (illustrative only, no concrete feature exists yet):

    payment (module) > refund (feature) > automatic-refund (variant)

  A feature SHOULD declare: purpose, owning module, dependencies, and
  the chain type it runs under (bax chains/chains.sdd -> Stages).


Component:

  A component represents an implementation boundary inside a feature —
  the discipline-level slice of code that actually realizes it.

  MAY include (reusing this project's own established layer vocabulary,
  bax ./architecture.sdd -> Layers, chains/chains.sdd -> Stages):

    DB    database / persistence
    BE    backend / domain logic
    API   API surface
    FE    frontend
    MD    mobile
    QA    tests
    DO    deployment / devops

  A component SHOULD declare: path, owner, responsibility, exposes,
  depends_on (bax CodeMapping below).


Ownership:

  Every project element (domain, module, feature, component) MUST have
  one clear owner.

  Example:

    payments (module)
      owns: payment state, payment rules, payment database model

    refund (feature)
      owns: refund behavior, refund workflow


Navigation:

  AI SHOULD resolve, top to bottom, before reading source code:

    Project > Domain > Module > Feature > Component > Code

  Entry point for this resolution: ./map.sdd (routing only, bax its own
  Purpose) and ./indexes.sdd (category-level fast lookup).


Rules:

  [PM1]
  Every project domain MUST have a project model file (bax
  ./domains.sdd -> Rules for the domain-level requirement this refines).

  [PM2]
  Every domain SHOULD expose its boundaries and declare owned
  components.

  [PM3]
  Project elements (domain/module/feature/component declarations) MUST
  NOT duplicate information owned by global SDD systems (chains, tasks,
  decisions, state, skills) — reference them instead.

  [PM4]
  Feature declarations MUST reference the chain type they run under
  (bax chains/chains.sdd -> Stages) instead of redefining chain
  lifecycle rules.

  [PM5]
  Feature/component declarations MUST reference task and decision rules
  (bax tasks/tasks.sdd, decisions/decisions.sdd) instead of redefining
  their lifecycle.

  [PM6]
  Component code paths MUST be mapped to project ownership (bax
  CodeMapping below).

  [PM7]
  AI SHOULD use this project model to locate code before searching the
  source tree (bax Navigation above).

  [PM8]
  This file MUST NOT redeclare the concrete domain/module list already
  owned by domains.sdd/modules.sdd (bax DoesNotOwn — avoids
  NoDuplication violations, bax architecture/architecture.sdd ->
  Principles).

  [PM9]
  Populating this hierarchy's layers (domain/module/feature/component
  entries) beyond what current analysis justifies is FORBIDDEN — bax
  ./instantiation.sdd -> [PI3], CorePrinciple.


CodeMapping:

  Every component SHOULD declare:

    path
    owner
    responsibility
    exposes
    depends_on

  (Real paths are lookup references for a future real project tree —
  bax PROJECT.sdd -> Note, prompt/new/14.md: creating backend/frontend/
  mobile/qa/devops directories now is explicitly out of scope.)


Portability:

  This file MUST remain reusable across projects unchanged — it defines
  the SHAPE of the hierarchy, not this project's concrete domains.
  Concrete instances belong to domains.sdd/modules.sdd and future
  project/<domain>/ content, not here. How much of this SHAPE a given
  project ends up populating is likewise reusable logic, owned by
  ./instantiation.sdd, not by this file.


Documentation:

  A project instance MAY declare its human-facing documentation structure
  so AI can keep docs synchronized with code changes and humans can
  navigate the system without reading .sdd/. This file defines the
  documentation SHAPE only — concrete docs belong to the project's
  ./docs/ directory (bax ../PROJECT.sdd -> Directories -> docs).

  LayerStructure:

    docs/
    ├── 00-about/        (project.md, glossary.md, system-overview.md)
    ├── 10-business/     (business-model.md, business-rules.md, domains/, workflows/)
    ├── 20-architecture/ (overview.md, decisions.md, boundaries.md, dependencies.md, diagrams/)
    ├── 30-backend/      (overview.md, modules/, services/, api/, diagrams/)
    ├── 40-frontend/     (overview.md, modules/, state/, diagrams/)
    ├── 50-mobile/       (overview.md, android/, ios/, diagrams/)
    ├── 60-database/     (overview.md, schema/, migrations/, indexing/, diagrams/)
    ├── 70-api/          (overview.md, contracts/, authentication.md, diagrams/)
    ├── 80-qa/           (strategy.md, bdd/, automation/, ui/, manual/, diagrams/)
    ├── 90-security/     (overview.md, threats.md, policies.md, incidents/)
    ├── 100-devops/      (overview.md, docker/, ci-cd/, deployment/, rollback/, diagrams/)
    ├── 110-infrastructure/ (overview.md, vps/, aws/, azure/, gcp/, kubernetes/)
    ├── 120-support/     (overview.md, runbooks/, troubleshooting/, incidents/, faq/)
    └── 130-operations/  (monitoring.md, alerting.md, sla.md, disaster-recovery.md)

  OwnershipMatrix:

    Every problem type MUST map to a primary owner, secondary owner,
    and runbook. This matrix lives at docs/00-about/ownership.md and
    is the human-facing lookup for "who do I call when X breaks?".

    Example:

      | Problem            | Primary | Secondary | Runbook           |
      | API 500            | BE      | DO        | api-500           |
      | UI broken          | FE      | QA        | ui-failure        |
      | Mobile crash       | MD      | QA        | mobile-crash      |
      | DB unavailable     | DB      | DO        | database-down     |
      | Deployment fail    | DO      | BE        | deployment-failure|
      | Security issue     | SEC     | DO        | security-incident |
      | Business rule wrong| Product | BE        | business-rule     |
      | Test failure       | QA      | Owner     | test-failure      |

  DocMetadata:

    Every doc file SHOULD declare its ownership so humans can identify
    the responsible team at a glance:

      Owner:       Backend
      Secondary:   Database, QA, Security
      Business:    Payment
      Related:     @PAY/refund, @DB/payment, @API/refund

    Every doc file SHOULD declare its intended audience:

      Audience:
        business
        developer
        qa
        devops
        support

    This lets AI route humans to the right doc without making them read
    everything (bax TokenEconomy in ../architecture/architecture.sdd
    -> Principles).

  OnePageSummary:

    Every major domain or component SHOULD have a one-page summary
    before detailed documentation:

      Purpose
      Owner
      Users
      Inputs
      Outputs
      Dependencies
      Main Flow
      Failure modes
      Current status

    Humans MUST NOT need to read 10 pages to understand a module's
    purpose and ownership.

  DiagramMetadata:

    Every diagram file SHOULD declare its metadata above the diagram:

      Layer:     ARCH
      Owner:     Backend
      Related:   @PAY, @DB/payment, @API/payment, @DO/payment
      Source:    .sdd/project/flows.sdd
      Generated: 2026-08-20
      Status:    ✓ verified

  CanonicalDiagramTypes:

    #CTX   Context
    #ARCH  Architecture
    #FLOW  Flow
    #SEQ   Sequence
    #DATA  Data model
    #DEP   Dependency
    #NET   Network
    #DEPLOY Deployment
    #CI    CI/CD
    #OBS   Observability
    #SEC   Security
    #TEST  Test flow

  Views:

    A single canonical relationship model in .sdd/project/flows.sdd
    MAY have multiple human-facing views. Each layer reads the same
    model but renders only the slice relevant to its concern (bax
    TokenEconomy in ../architecture/architecture.sdd -> Principles).
    Examples: Business view, Backend view, Frontend view, Mobile view,
    QA view, DevOps view.

  DocIndex:

    docs/index.md is the human entry point. It MUST list every layer
    with its Owner, Purpose, Related domains, Related diagrams, and
    Related runbooks.

  DocStatus:

    Documentation status is tracked per work item using compact symbols
    (bax ../tasks/tasks.sdd -> DocumentationStatus):

      DOC+  documentation current and verified
      DOC~  documentation stale, update required
      DOC!  documentation missing, block VR

    A work item MUST NOT pass VR while any impacted layer's
    documentation is DOC! or DOC~ without an approved exception.


RiskRegister:

  A project instance MAY maintain a risk register so architecture and
  implementation decisions can account for known risks. This file defines
  the schema only — concrete risks belong to the instantiated project.

  Schema:

    id
    area
    risk
    probability
    impact
    trigger
    mitigation
    owner

  Example:

    #RISK-004

    Area:
      Database

    Risk:
      PostgreSQL becomes bottleneck

    Probability:
      medium

    Impact:
      high

    Trigger:
      CPU > 75%

    Mitigation:
      indexing
      caching
      read replica

    Owner:
      DB / DO

  AI MUST consult the risk register when proposing architecture or
  implementation changes (bax ../architecture/principles.sdd ->
  ArchitectureSelection).


ResolutionOrder:

  PROJECT > DOMAIN > MODULE > FEATURE > COMPONENT > CODE


NavigationFiles:

  map.sdd:           Project navigation map (routing only)
  architecture.sdd:   This project's concrete architecture
  flows.sdd:          This project's execution flow (bax [PM4])
  dependencies.sdd:    Concrete dependency graph (schema currently)
  indexes.sdd:         Category-level fast navigation index
  domains.sdd:         Domain schema and concrete domain list
  modules.sdd:         Module schema and concrete module list
  instantiation.sdd:   How a project instance gets decided (STEP 15)


CanonicalSections:

  The .sdd/ Architecture v1 defines 15 canonical sections. This file
  does not implement them all — it references them so AI knows the
  target shape when reading or extending .sdd/:

    01 PROJECT        — PROJECT.sdd, project model
    02 PROTOCOL       — syntax, states, stages, rules
    03 SYMBOLS        — compact glyphs and meanings
    04 SKILLS         — engineering skill catalog
    05 PROJECT MODEL  — domains, modules, features, components
    06 FLOWS / CHAINS — execution chains and dependencies
    07 TASKS          — task engine and instances
    08 DECISIONS      — human decision lifecycle
    09 SECURITY       — security as cross-cutting gate
    10 VERIFICATION   — QA, review, VR
    11 STATE          — state notation engine
    12 DOCS CONTRACT  — docs/ structure and ownership
    13 COST / CONTEXT — progressive context loading
    14 DRIFT          — code-vs-model drift detection
    15 RISK           — risk register and health

  These sections are distributed across the existing .sdd/ files; no
  new file is created for each section (NoDuplication). AI MUST load
  them in the order above when doing full project discovery (bax
  ProgressiveContextLoading in ../tasks/tasks.sdd).


Domain:

  A domain represents a coherent business capability. This file defines
  the schema only — concrete domain declarations live at .sdd/project/
  <domain>/PROJECT.sdd once real project analysis justifies them (bax
  ./instantiation.sdd -> CorePrinciple).

  Schema:

    ID:
      @<domain>

    Type:
      domain

    Purpose:
      <one-line description>

    Paths:
      BE:  <backend path>
      FE:  <frontend path>
      MD:  <mobile path>
      DB:  <database path>
      QA:  <test path>

    Owns:
      <list of capabilities>

    Uses:
      @<other-domain>

    API:
      @api/<domain>

    Data:
      @db/<domain>

    Chain:
      <domain-specific chain, or global default>

    Risk:
      <R0-R5>

    Security:
      @SC/<domain>

    Docs:
      @docs/business/<domain>
      @docs/developer/<domain>


Module:

  A module represents a technical or functional boundary inside a
  domain. This file defines the schema only — concrete module
  declarations live at .sdd/project/<domain>/<module>.sdd once real
  project analysis justifies them.

  Schema:

    ID:
      @<domain>/<module>

    Type:
      module

    Purpose:
      <one-line description>

    Paths:
      BE:  <backend path>
      FE:  <frontend path>
      MD:  <mobile path>
      DB:  <database path>
      QA:  <test path>

    Owns:
      <list of responsibilities>

    DependsOn:
      @<other-module>

    UsedBy:
      @<other-module>

    API:
      @api/<module>

    Data:
      @db/<module>

    Chain:
      <module-specific chain>

    Risk:
      <R0-R5>

    Security:
      @SC/<module>

    Docs:
      @docs/business/<module>
      @docs/developer/<module>


Component:

  A component represents an implementation boundary inside a module —
  the discipline-level slice of code that actually realizes it.

  Schema:

    ID:
      @<domain>/<module>/<component>

    Type:
      component

    Stage:
      BE / DB / API / FE / MD / QA / DO

    Path:
      ./<layer>/<path>

    Owns:
      <list of files/responsibilities>

    Tests:
      ./<test-path>

    API:
      @api/<component>

    DB:
      @db/<component>

    Skills:
      @<stage>/<skill>

    DependsOn:
      @<other-component>

    UsedBy:
      @<other-component>

    Chain:
      <component-specific chain>

    Risk:
      <R0-R5>

    Security:
      @SC/<component>


SourceMapping:

  Source code artifacts are mapped to semantic identifiers through
  mirror files, not through source code annotations (bax CleanSource
  in ../architecture/architecture.sdd -> Principles).

  Mirror file format:
    Own:      <what this artifact owns>
    Do:       <what this artifact does>
    Use:      <what this artifact uses>
    Test:     <test paths>
    ->        <pointer to module/domain model>

  Mirror files live at .sdd/project/<source-path>/<artifact>.sdd
  and are created only when real source exists.

  Bidirectional resolution:
    source -> semantic: mirror file path -> semantic ID
    semantic -> source: semantic ID -> mirror file path -> real source


Declared:

  Approved project intent — architecture, domains, modules, components,
  flows, decisions, constraints.

Observed:

  Actual repository/runtime state — source code, tests, config, metrics,
  actual dependencies.

Drift:

  When Declared != Observed, AI MUST create a DRIFT finding and a task
  to reconcile the difference. Drift severity:

    D0 = no drift
    D1 = minor (typo, naming)
    D2 = significant (missing module, wrong dependency)
    D3 = critical (architecture violation, security gap)

  AI MUST detect drift by comparing change impact against the project
  model, not by re-reading the entire repository (bax ProgressiveContextLoading
  in ../tasks/tasks.sdd, TokenEconomy in ../architecture/architecture.sdd
  -> Principles).


MinimalModel:

  Every domain/component does NOT need a 500-line document. The minimal
  model is:

    ID
    Paths
    Owns
    DependsOn / UsedBy
    Chain
    Risk

  Everything else lives in the graph and skills. This prevents context
  bloat and keeps the project model token-efficient.


Graph:

  The project graph is the relationship layer on top of the project model.
  It answers: "What is related to what?" and "Who owns what?" The project
  model says "@payments exists"; the graph says "@payments -> @users,
  @payments -> @gateway, @payments/refund owner: BE".

  The graph has three sub-types (see graph/graph.sdd):

    DependencyGraph  — "What is A dependent on?"
    ImpactGraph      — "What is affected when A changes?"
    OwnershipGraph   — "Who is responsible for this work?"

  Graph structure:

    Nodes:     semantic identifiers (@payments, @payments/refund, ...)
    Edges:     relationships between nodes with metadata
    Ownership: node -> owner mapping

  Edge metadata:

    type:        dependency | calls | uses | writes | tests |
                 secures | deploys | verifies | produces | consumes
    protocol:    HTTP | gRPC | CLI | Event | ...
    contract:    @api/refund
    risk:        R0-R5
    required:    true | false
    direction:   inbound | outbound
    since:       <version or date>
    status:      + | ~ | ! | ?

  Graph is the primary source of truth for relationships. AI MUST NOT
  use filesystem search when the graph already contains the answer
  (bax TokenEconomy in ../architecture/architecture.sdd -> Principles).


GraphDrift:

  Graph drift is a subset of project drift (bax Declared/Observed/Drift
  above). When the graph says:

    @payments/refund -> @db/refunds

  but the actual code has:

    @payments/refund -> @redis/cache

  AI MUST:
    1. Detect the drift
    2. Create a DRIFT finding
    3. Create a task to reconcile the graph
    4. NOT silently update the graph without human approval for
       significant changes (bax ../PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision)

  Drift severity:
    D0 = no drift
    D1 = minor (new optional dependency)
    D2 = significant (missing dependency, wrong owner)
    D3 = critical (security gap, architecture violation)


FallbackDiscovery:

  When the graph says nothing about a node, AI MUST fall back to
  discovery, but MUST NOT silently update the graph:

    GRAPH MISSING
      ↓
    DISCOVER
      ↓
    OBSERVE
      ↓
    DRAFT UPDATE
      ↓
    HUMAN APPROVAL (if significant)
      ↓
    UPDATE GRAPH

  Domain-local graph updates MAY be proposed automatically, but MUST
  be reviewed before being accepted (bax ../PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision).


DomainGraph:

  Each domain MAY have its own local graph at .sdd/project/<domain>/
  graph.sdd containing only the nodes and edges relevant to that domain.
  The global graph at .sdd/graph/graph.sdd contains only high-level
  domain-to-domain relationships.

  Resolution order:
    1. Global graph (.sdd/graph/graph.sdd)
    2. Domain graph (.sdd/project/<domain>/graph.sdd)
    3. Component model (.sdd/project/<domain>/<component>.sdd)
    4. Fallback discovery (if all graphs are silent)

  This keeps the global graph small and the domain graph detailed —
  context locality (bax TokenEconomy in ../architecture/architecture.sdd
  -> Principles).


State: STEP 4 (domains/modules) + STEP 14 (this file, Feature/Component)
  + STEP 15 (Purpose/DoesNotOwn/[PM9]/Portability/NavigationFiles
  cross-references to instantiation.sdd)
  + — expanded Domain/Module/Component schemas
  with Paths/Uses/API/Data/Chain/Risk/Security/Docs, added SourceMapping,
  Declared/Observed/Drift, MinimalModel, Project/Root entry point at
  project/PROJECT.sdd
  + — added Graph: section (DependencyGraph,
  ImpactGraph, OwnershipGraph, EdgeMetadata, GraphDrift, FallbackDiscovery,
  DomainGraph), created graph/graph.sdd as global graph entry point

Note:
  (prompt/new/36.md — source labeled this "STEP 13", a THIRD consecutive
  reuse of a step number already used by prompt/new/34.md and its own
  prior chunk 35.md; this repo's own sequential count treats it as
  STEP 14, bax ../PROJECT.sdd -> Note.)

  Naming/placement decision: the source proposes this content as
  `project.sdd`, plus full rewrites of `map.sdd`, `dependencies.sdd` and
  `indexes.sdd`. All three of those already exist in this repo with
  different, STEP-4-era content (bax their own Spec headers and Notes,
  prompt/new/21.md) that already fully satisfies their stated scope —
  overwriting them would lose STEP 4's reset history and violate this
  repo's own NoDuplication/SingleSourceOfTruth principles for no gain.
  They were left untouched; this file's `NavigationFiles` section
  references them instead of restating their content (bax [PM8]).

  Rule IDs: the source used a bare `[P1]-[P10]` range, which collides
  with `prompts/prompts.sdd`'s own `[P#]` range (bax PROJECT.sdd ->
  Directories -> prompts). Renumbered to `[PM1]-[PM8]`
  ("ProjectModel") to read unambiguously next to this repo's other
  prefixes ([R] PROJECT rules, [C] chains, [T] tasks, [D] decisions,
  [S] state, [AP] architecture principles, [P] prompts). Two of the
  source's ten rules ([P4]/skills-reference and [P9]/CodeMapping-only)
  were folded into neighboring rules rather than kept as separates,
  since they restated the same NoDuplication point already covered.

  Deliberate departures from the source spec, per the standing "apply,
  don't copy" rule: Domain/Module sections were shortened to pointers
  into the already-existing `domains.sdd`/`modules.sdd` rather than
  redefining them here (avoids a second source of truth); Component's
  "MAY include: backend/api/frontend/mobile/database/qa/devops" prose
  was replaced with this project's own already-established DB/BE/API/
  FE/MD/QA/DO vocabulary (bax project/architecture.sdd -> Layers,
  chains/chains.sdd -> Stages) instead of introducing a second,
  differently-worded component list; added an explicit DoesNotOwn
  section (absent from the source) to make the boundary against the
  five sibling files explicit, matching this repo's established
  per-file convention (bax architecture/principles.sdd -> DoesNotOwn).

  STEP 15 addendum (prompt/new/37.md): this file's fixed-hierarchy
  framing was in tension with 37.md's "PROJECT IS GENERATED, NOT
  PREDEFINED" principle — resolved by cross-referencing the new
  ./instantiation.sdd (Purpose, DoesNotOwn, new [PM9], Portability,
  NavigationFiles all updated) rather than editing ProjectStructure or
  weakening Portability's "reusable across projects unchanged" claim:
  the SHAPE stays fixed, only HOW MUCH of it gets populated is now
  explicitly owned elsewhere. Bax ./instantiation.sdd -> Note for the
  full STEP 15 rationale, including the source's fourth consecutive
  "STEP 13" mislabel.

  addendum (prompt/new/60.md): Domain/Module/Component schemas
   expanded with Paths, Uses, API, Data, Chain, Risk, Security, Docs;
   SourceMapping section added (mirror files, bidirectional resolution);
   Declared/Observed/Drift model added; MinimalModel principle added;
   project/PROJECT.sdd referenced as root entry point (Windows filesystem
   limitation: project.sdd and PROJECT.sdd cannot coexist in the same
   directory, so root project model metadata is referenced from here
   rather than duplicated).
  added Decisions: section (decision storage
  under project/<domain>/decisions/<state>/, decision index, decision
  retrieval flow, decision impact workflow, token economy for decisions,
  relation model DependsOn/Supersedes/Conflicts/Affects/DerivedFrom/
  Requires/ReviewedBy/ImplementedBy/VerifiedBy).

Decisions:

  A project instance MAY maintain decisions at the domain level so AI can
  trace why a choice was made and what it affects. This file defines the
  SHAPE only — concrete decisions belong to the instantiated project.

  Storage:
    project/<domain>/decisions/<state>/*.sdd

    Where <state> is one of: pending / approved / rejected / superseded

    Example:
      project/<domain>/decisions/pending/DEC-<ID>.sdd
      project/<domain>/decisions/approved/DEC-<ID>.sdd

  DecisionIndex:
    Each domain MAY maintain an INDEX.sdd for fast lookup:

      Domain: @<domain>
      Active: DEC-<ID>, DEC-<ID>
      Review: DEC-<ID>
      Superseded: DEC-<ID>, DEC-<ID>

    AI MUST load INDEX first, then only the relevant decisions.

  DecisionRetrieval:
    Task -> Domain -> Decision INDEX -> affected decisions -> only relevant DEC files

  DecisionImpactWorkflow:
    NEW DECISION
      -> COMPARE
      -> CONFLICT CHECK
      -> IMPACT ANALYSIS
        -> SKILL IMPACT
        -> GRAPH IMPACT
        -> TASK IMPACT
        -> DOC IMPACT
      -> HUMAN GATE
      -> APPROVE
      -> CREATE / UPDATE TASKS

  RelationModel:
    DependsOn:     decision depends on another decision
    Supersedes:    decision replaces an older decision
    Conflicts:     decision contradicts another decision
    Affects:       decision affects a project element
    DerivedFrom:   decision derived from another decision
    Requires:      decision requires a skill
    ReviewedBy:    decision reviewed by
    ImplementedBy: decision implemented by a task
    VerifiedBy:    decision verified by a QA task

  TokenEconomy:
    ->  affects
    =>  produces
    <-  depends
    ~   pending
    +   accepted
    !   conflict/fail
    x   superseded
    @   review

  Decision states:
    ~ proposed
    @ review
    + accepted
    ! rejected
    x superseded
    ? deprecated


